{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution for $\\lambda$ given Poisson observations $X_1, \\dots, X_n$ and a Gamma prior $\\text{Gamma}(\\alpha, \\beta)$ is a Gamma distribution with updated parameters: $\\lambda | X_1, \\dots, X_n \\sim \\text{Gamma}(\\alpha + \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i, \\, \\beta + n)$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [03:17:17\u201303:18:48]: 'we showed that our p lambda conditioned on x because yeah i here wrote some not very beautiful things we can show better that it's proportional to exponent minus n plus b lambda and lambda a plus some xi minus one. That's what we showed. And from it, we can say like, oh, it's very similar to gamma distribution, which is some b and lambda a n minus one, where a n is this one, b n is this one.' Also, [03:18:48\u201303:19:18]: 'we can compute that the denominator integral of this is exactly the inverse constant. So our real distribution just multiplied on this constant.' This shows the posterior is proportional to a Gamma distribution with shape $\\alpha + \\sum X_i$ and rate $\\beta + n$."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The joint distribution is proportional to $\\prod_{i=1}^n \\frac{\\lambda^{X_i} e^{-\\lambda}}{X_i!} \\times \\frac{\\beta^\\alpha}{\\Gamma(\\alpha)} \\lambda^{\\alpha-1} e^{-\\beta \\lambda}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [03:12:43\u201303:13:28]: 'from 1 to n, the xi conditioned on lambda, on the lambda, what is it? First, I think we already calculated this above. It's the xi and minus lambda n multiplied on lambda xi divided by product of Xi factorial, yeah? And multiply it on prior, on the gamma function. It is of the A divided by gamma function on A, and A minus one, exponent minus B lambda.' This matches the joint distribution expression."
    },
    {
      "claim": "After integrating out $\\lambda$, the posterior is proportional to $\\lambda^{\\alpha + \\sum X_i - 1} e^{-(\\beta + n)\\lambda}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [03:15:30\u201303:16:06]: 'exponent minus lambda n. Yes, exponent minus lambda n. Lambda sum xi. Then, product of factorial collapse, gamma function, and b in the a degree collapses. Also, lambda i minus 1 exponent minus minus one, sorry, not the best one. Exponent minus b lambda.' This shows the exponent and power terms, and [03:17:17\u201303:17:47] confirms the exponent is $-(\\beta + n)\\lambda$ and the power is $\\alpha + \\sum X_i - 1$."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution has shape parameter $\\alpha + \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i$ and rate parameter $\\beta + n$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [03:17:17\u201303:17:47]: 'so we just add xi we just add b plus n and other things are constant so in fact we showed that our p lambda conditioned on x because yeah i here wrote some not very beautiful things we can show better that it's proportional to exponent minus n plus b lambda and lambda a plus some xi minus one.' This indicates the shape is $\\alpha + \\sum X_i$ and rate is $\\beta + n$."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The Gamma distribution is defined as $\\text{Gamma}(\\alpha, \\beta) \\sim \\frac{\\beta^\\alpha}{\\Gamma(\\alpha)} \\lambda^{\\alpha-1} e^{-\\beta \\lambda}$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [03:13:30\u201303:14:10]: 'It is of the A divided by gamma function on A, and A minus one, exponent minus B lambda.' This matches the Gamma distribution definition."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is proportional to the Gamma distribution with updated parameters",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [03:18:48\u201303:19:18]: 'we can compute that the denominator integral of this is exactly the inverse constant. So our real distribution just multiplied on this constant.' This shows the posterior is a Gamma distribution with the updated parameters."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The joint distribution simplifies to a Gamma distribution after marginalization",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [03:19:51\u201303:20:27]: 'and compute everything that depends on lambda. Then find the distribution that has similar lambda theme and just add the constant and say that the distribution is the same.' This shows the posterior is a Gamma distribution after marginalization."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is derived using the Bayesian update method with Poisson observations and Gamma prior",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "From [03:10:59\u201303:11:32]: 'We have independent variables from Poisson. We want to, instead of maximum likelihood, we want to compute bias and estimate on lambda. Also we have some prior from gamma distribution.' This confirms the Bayesian update method is used."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 3,
  "caveat": "The speaker's explanation is somewhat disorganized with frequent pauses and corrections, making it slightly unclear. The exact notation for the posterior parameters is mentioned as uncertain, but the overall derivation is clear enough to extract the key claims."
}
